    │
  1 │ Architecture Decision Record: Event-Driven Order Pipeline
    │
  3 │ Status: Accepted Date: 2024-12-01 Authors: Platform Team
    │
    │
  7 │ Context
    │
  9 │ Our monolithic order processing system handles ~50k orders/day with synchronous HTTP calls between services. During Black Friday 2023, cascading timeouts caused a 47-minute outage affecting $2.3M in lost revenue. The system cannot scale horizontally because:
    │
 11 │ 1. Tight coupling: The order service directly calls payment, inventory, and notification services
 12 │ 2. No backpressure: A slow downstream service blocks the entire pipeline
 13 │ 3. No retry semantics: Failed payments require manual intervention
 14 │ 4. Limited observability: No distributed tracing across service boundaries
    │
    │
 16 │ Decision
    │
 18 │ We will migrate to an event-driven architecture using Apache Kafka as the message backbone, with the following topology:
    │
    │
 20 │   ┌─────────────┐    ┌───────┐    ┌──────────────────┐
 21 │   │  API Gateway │───▶│ Kafka │───▶│  Order Service   │
 22 │   └─────────────┘    │       │    └──────────────────┘
 23 │                      │       │            │
 24 │                      │       │    ┌───────▼──────────┐
 25 │                      │       │◀───│ Payment Service  │
 26 │                      │       │    └──────────────────┘
 27 │                      │       │            │
 28 │                      │       │    ┌───────▼──────────┐
 29 │                      │       │◀───│ Inventory Service│
 30 │                      │       │    └──────────────────┘
 31 │                      │       │            │
 32 │                      │       │    ┌───────▼──────────┐
 33 │                      │       │    │ Notification Svc │
 34 │                      └───────┘    └──────────────────┘
    │
    │
 38 │ Key Design Choices
    │
    │
 40 │ Decision           │ Choice                              │ Alternatives Considered
    │ ───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────
 40 │ Message broker     │ Kafka                               │ RabbitMQ, AWS SQS, NATS
 40 │ Serialization      │ Protobuf + Schema Registry          │ JSON, Avro
 40 │ Delivery guarantee │ Exactly-once (idempotent consumers) │ At-least-once
 40 │ Partition strategy │ By  customer_id                     │ By  order_id , random
 40 │ Consumer groups    │ One per service                     │ Shared consumers
    │
    │
 48 │ Event Schema
    │
 50 │ Orders flow through these states via events:
    │
 52 │ -  OrderCreated  - Initial order placement
 53 │ -  PaymentRequested  - Sent to payment service
 54 │ -  PaymentConfirmed  /  PaymentFailed  - Payment result
 55 │ -  InventoryReserved  /  InventoryInsufficient  - Stock check
 56 │ -  OrderConfirmed  - All checks passed
 57 │ -  OrderShipped  - Fulfillment complete (Phase 2)
 58 │ -  OrderDelivered  - Delivery confirmed (Phase 2)
    │
    │
 60 │ Retry & Dead Letter Policy
    │
    │
 62 │   retry:
 63 │     max_attempts: 5
 64 │     backoff: exponential
 65 │     base_delay: 1s
 66 │     max_delay: 60s
 67 │     dead_letter_topic: "orders.dlq"
 68 │     alert_on_dlq: true
    │
    │
 72 │ Consequences
    │
    │
 74 │ Positive
    │
 76 │ - Decoupled services: Each service can be deployed, scaled, and fail independently
 77 │ - Natural backpressure: Kafka consumer lag absorbs traffic spikes without cascading failures
 78 │ - Audit trail: Kafka log provides a complete, ordered history of all state transitions
 79 │ - Replay capability: Can replay events from any offset to rebuild state or debug issues
 80 │ - Horizontal scaling: Add consumer instances to scale throughput linearly
    │
    │
 82 │ Negative
    │
 84 │ - Eventual consistency: Orders are no longer confirmed synchronously; UI must handle pending states
 85 │ - Operational complexity: Kafka cluster requires dedicated ops expertise (ZooKeeper/KRaft, partition rebalancing, ISR monitoring)
 86 │ - Debugging difficulty: Distributed traces across async events are harder to follow than synchronous call stacks
 87 │ - Schema evolution: Breaking changes to event schemas require careful versioning and migration
    │
    │
 89 │ Risks & Mitigations
    │
    │
 91 │ Risk                          │ Likelihood │ Impact   │ Mitigation
    │ ──────────────────────────────┼────────────┼──────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 91 │ Kafka cluster failure         │ Low        │ Critical │ Multi-AZ deployment, 3x replication, automated failover
 91 │ Consumer lag during spikes    │ Medium     │ High     │ Auto-scaling consumer groups, lag alerting at 10k threshold
 91 │ Poison pill messages          │ Medium     │ Medium   │ Schema validation, DLQ with alerting, circuit breaker per topic
 91 │ Data loss on unclean shutdown │ Low        │ Critical │  acks=all ,  min.insync.replicas=2 , idempotent producer
    │
    │
 98 │ Performance Targets
    │
    │
100 │ > Note: All latency targets are P99 measured at the API gateway.
    │
    │
102 │ - Order placement to confirmation: < 5 seconds (currently 12s synchronous)
103 │ - Payment processing: < 3 seconds (P99, excluding bank-side latency)
104 │ - Throughput: > 500 orders/sec sustained (10x current peak)
105 │ - Consumer lag: < 1000 messages during normal operation
106 │ - Recovery time: < 30 seconds after consumer restart
    │
    │
108 │ References
    │
110 │ 1. Designing Event-Driven Systems (https://www.confluent.io/designing-event-driven-systems/) - Ben Stopford
111 │ 2. Kafka: The Definitive Guide (https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/kafka-the-definitive/9781492043072/) - Shapira et al.
112 │ 3. Internal post-mortem:  INC-2024-BF-001  (Black Friday 2023 outage)
113 │ 4. Prototype benchmark results:  docs/benchmarks/kafka-pilot-2024q3.pdf
    │
117 │ This ADR supersedes ADR-017: Synchronous Order Pipeline (adr-017.md) from 2022-03.
    │
